lucidos-icon-themes (0.4.4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * An icon for GNOME Console, which is the terminal LucidOS ships now. The
    theme had org.gnome.Terminal and foot; Console asks for
    org.gnome.Console, so swapping the terminal in the image left the dock
    falling through to Adwaita's generic black prompt -- the one icon in the
    dock that was not LucidOS's.
  * The icon-theme.cache files are regenerated. They are committed rather than
    built at install time, and a stale cache is authoritative: an icon added
    beside one is an icon GTK does not see.

 -- Javid Medina <javidamedina@gmail.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 22:40:00 -0400

lucidos-icon-themes (0.4.3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Icons for foot and Resources, the terminal and the system monitor LucidOS
    actually ships. Both were showing the generic application icon in
    LucidOS's own dock, which is the one place a missing icon is not
    somebody else's problem.
  * Symlinks to the existing Lucid terminal and Mission Center artwork
    rather than new drawings. Inventing icons in somebody else's visual
    language is how a theme stops looking like one theme, and the roles are
    the same: foot is a terminal, Resources is a system monitor.

 -- Javid Medina <javidamedina@gmail.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 05:55:00 -0400

lucidos-icon-themes (0.4.2) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The settings icon is named lucid-settings. It is LucidOS's own artwork --
    a gear and a wrench in Lucid's teal -- and it was filed under
    org.gnome.Settings, which is the name of somebody else's application. The
    old name stays as a symlink, so the theme still dresses GNOME Settings
    for anyone using it on GNOME: the artwork was never GNOME's, but that
    name was the only way it reached the settings window there.

 -- Javid Medina <javidamedina@gmail.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 05:30:00 -0400

lucidos-icon-themes (0.4.1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Targets resolute. 0.3.0 is published in ppa:javidmedina/lucidos for
    noble, which is the series LucidOS has left: the image is 26.04 and
    anything uploaded now builds for resolute regardless of what the old
    entry says.
  * No icon changes. This is the 0.4.0 tree, which split the themes along
    the licence boundary and fixed Lucid-Dark's inheritance, uploaded to a
    series that can actually use it.

 -- Javid Medina <javidamedina@gmail.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 23:40:00 -0400

lucidos-icon-themes (0.4.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Lucid-Light is now Lucid, and Lucid-Everything sits beneath it as the
    fallback for the applications LucidOS does not draw icons for.
  * Split into two binary packages along the licence boundary:
    lucidos-icon-themes (Lucid, Lucid-Dark, CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0) and
    lucid-everything-icon-theme (GPL-3.0). The first depends on the second,
    because Lucid inherits it: without it every third-party icon falls
    through to hicolor.
  * debian/install named Lucid-Light*, which had been renamed and so matched
    nothing. The package installed no icons at all.
  * index.theme declared 78 directories per theme, of which eleven existed,
    and ended in a malformed "16x16/" entry that made GTK warn at every
    application start. Six directories that do exist -- the scalable and
    symbolic ones -- were never declared, so LucidOS's own folder, user-home
    and user-trash icons lost to the theme they inherit.
  * Removed 11,482 AppleDouble and .DS_Store files, 45 MB, which dh_install
    was copying into the package.
  * The themes are checked at package build time: every declared directory
    must exist and contain icons, the index must parse through GKeyFile, and
    no file may be zero-filled or be macOS metadata.

 -- Javid Medina <javidamedina@gmail.com>  Sat, 05 Sep 2026 23:10:00 -0400

